{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q021_nad_binding_convergent_evolution","answer":"In stable SCOPe 2.08, NAD(P)-binding domains are not one evolutionary group: the NAD(P)-binding Rossmann-fold superfamily c.2.1 (SUNID 51735), FAD/NAD(P)-binding superfamily c.3.1 (SUNID 51905), and DHS-like NAD/FAD-binding superfamily c.31.1 (SUNID 52467) belong to distinct folds c.2, c.3, and c.31, respectively. They share only class c, “Alpha and beta proteins (a/b),” which denotes broad architecture. Within a SCOPe superfamily, probable common ancestry and subsequent divergence are supported; a shared family would indicate a closer relationship. Across these different folds and superfamilies, SCOPe makes no homology assertion. Their recurrence is consistent with convergent acquisition of NAD(P)-binding, but different SCOPe placement alone does not prove independent origin or convergence, nor exclude very remote divergence.

Cofactor binding is insufficient evidence of homology because NAD and NADP impose common chemical and geometric constraints that can independently favor similar pockets, glycine-rich loops, phosphate contacts, or catalytic arrangements. Active sites are small local features and can converge even when whole-domain topology differs. Conversely, homologous proteins can retain ancestry while their active sites and substrate preferences diverge. Establishing homology requires evidence extending across the domain—statistically significant sequence correspondence, global structural alignment with matching topology, conserved residues in equivalent structural contexts, compatible domain architecture, and phylogenetic or literature support. Arguing convergence additionally requires independent evidence that distinguishes separate origins from undetected remote homology.

Therefore NAD versus NADP preference, exact substrate, reaction chemistry, catalytic mechanism, pathway role, whole-protein function, and active-site residue equivalence must not be transferred solely from cofactor use or SCOPe fold placement. A cofactor-binding resemblance also does not make structures interchangeable templates: template selection requires compatible domain boundaries, sequence coverage, global structure, ligand/conformational state, and biological context.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/biological-interpretation.md","scop-homology/references/api-usage.md","scop-homology/scripts/scop_compare.py","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=NAD%28P%29-binding&limit=20","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=NAD-binding&limit=20","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=NAD&limit=100","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51735","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51735/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51905","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/51905/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52467","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/52467/parents"]}]}
